rows.en:
Ha ha ha.
Sorry for asking such a silly question.
That's right, it's the restroom.{p}

By the time I started at this school, he was already famous in certain circles as a legend.
The man who never went to the toilet.
The girls especially paid him a tremendous amount of attention.{p}

Takeuchi had come so close his breath was on my cheek.
Takeuchi's breath had a strong grassy smell.
That was when it happened.{p}

Tiny green bug-like things, writhing even inside Takeuchi's eyeballs.
I was too scared to move.
I'll never forget Takeuchi's face at that moment as long as I live.{p}

I see.
Then take a guess.
Where do you think it is?{p}

Do you really think so?
Don't tell me you get called to the faculty room a lot?
When it comes to the place everyone goes in and out of at school, it's got to be the restroom.{p}

...You don't know?
Oh well, I'll tell you.
It's the restroom.{p}

No, no.
You don't get it.
Sure, everyone passes through the school gate a lot, but...{p}

So I went around behind the old school building.
That day, too, the weather was fine. It was just right for sunbathing.
I poked just my head out from the shadow of the building and peeked at the spot where Takeuchi had been standing the other day.{p}

Takeuchi was glaring at me with a terrifying face.
Can you believe it?
His body had turned greenish, his hands were covered in leaves, and from his feet, hundreds of root-like things had come out and stabbed into the ground.{p}

I squeezed my eyes shut, trying not to look at Takeuchi.
And then, you know, a breath that smelled like raw green grass blew softly across my face.
It was so gross I got goosebumps.{p}

Ha ha ha...
Anyway, it was no situation for reciting poetry.
Go on, guess again.{p}
